At the same time, teenagers still need between eight and ten hours of sleep a night. If they fall asleep at midnight and have to get up at half past six for school, they lose several hours of sleep every night. Over a week, this ''sleep debt'' can affect their memory, their mood and their ability to concentrate in class.

Bright screens make the problem worse, because the light from phones and computers can delay the release of melatonin even further. Doctors therefore advise teenagers to switch off devices at least an hour before bed. Some schools in different countries have also started lessons later in the morning, and early studies suggest that their students arrive more alert and miss fewer classes.', NULL, '{"A": "Students go to bed even later.", "B": "Students are more alert and attend more classes.", "C": "Teachers are less tired.", "D": "Students get lower marks.", "E": "Parents are less worried."}'::jsonb, 'B', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «Students are more alert and attend more classes.».');

