Critics of this view argue that fast fashion has also made clothing affordable for millions of families who could not previously buy new clothes, and that the industry provides jobs in developing countries. Most experts agree, however, that consumers can reduce the damage without spending more: by buying fewer items of better quality, repairing clothes, and choosing second-hand shops. As one researcher put it, ''The most sustainable garment is the one already in your wardrobe.''', NULL, '{"A": "Fast fashion has made clothing more affordable and created jobs.", "B": "Fast fashion causes no environmental damage.", "C": "Cotton is worse for the environment than oil.", "D": "Consumers should stop buying clothes.", "E": "Second-hand shops are too expensive."}'::jsonb, 'A', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «Fast fashion has made clothing more affordable and created jobs.».');

A T-shirt that costs less than a sandwich seems like a bargain. But economists and environmental scientists point out that the price on the label rarely reflects the real cost of making it.

