Because the clothes are cheap, people buy more of them and wear each item fewer times. Many garments are thrown away after only a handful of uses, and only a small percentage of textiles are recycled into new clothing. The rest is burned or buried.

Critics of this view argue that fast fashion has also made clothing affordable for millions of families who could not previously buy new clothes, and that the industry provides jobs in developing countries. Most experts agree, however, that consumers can reduce the damage without spending more: by buying fewer items of better quality, repairing clothes, and choosing second-hand shops. As one researcher put it, ''The most sustainable garment is the one already in your wardrobe.''', NULL, '{"A": "cheap to produce", "B": "easy to wash", "C": "causing little long-term harm to the environment", "D": "fashionable and modern", "E": "made in a local factory"}'::jsonb, 'C', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «causing little long-term harm to the environment».');

