The practical lesson, then, is not that we should eliminate choice but that we should organise it. Grouping options into clear categories, or deciding on our most important criteria before we start comparing, can turn an overwhelming decision into a manageable one.', NULL, '{"A": "Shoppers dislike jam.", "B": "Supermarkets hide the best products.", "C": "People always choose the cheapest option.", "D": "Large displays are difficult to see.", "E": "Comparing many similar options requires a lot of effort."}'::jsonb, 'E', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «Comparing many similar options requires a lot of effort.».');

It seems obvious that more choice is better. If a shop offers thirty kinds of jam rather than six, surely customers are more likely to find exactly what they want. Yet a well-known experiment conducted in a supermarket suggested the opposite. When shoppers were shown a large display of jams, more of them stopped to look, but far fewer actually bought anything than when the display was small.

