It would be a mistake, however, to conclude that choice is simply harmful. Later attempts to repeat the jam study produced mixed results: in some situations a wide selection discouraged people, while in others it made no difference or even helped. The effect seems to depend on whether people already know what they prefer, how easy the options are to compare, and how much the decision matters to them.

The practical lesson, then, is not that we should eliminate choice but that we should organise it. Grouping options into clear categories, or deciding on our most important criteria before we start comparing, can turn an overwhelming decision into a manageable one.', NULL, '{"A": "They may imagine that a rejected option would have been better.", "B": "They did not compare any options.", "C": "The product was of poor quality.", "D": "Someone else made the decision for them.", "E": "They paid too much for it."}'::jsonb, 'A', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «They may imagine that a rejected option would have been better.».');

