These findings have serious implications for the legal system, which has traditionally placed great weight on the confident testimony of eyewitnesses. As a result, many police forces have changed how they interview witnesses, avoiding leading questions. The research does not mean that memory is worthless; most of what we remember is broadly accurate. It means that confidence alone is not proof of accuracy.', NULL, '{"A": "They avoid leading questions in interviews.", "B": "They interview witnesses with family members.", "C": "They no longer interview witnesses.", "D": "They ask witnesses to describe films.", "E": "They only use video evidence."}'::jsonb, 'A', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «They avoid leading questions in interviews.».');

We tend to think of memory as a recording: something happens, the brain stores it, and later we play it back. Decades of research suggest that this picture is misleading. Each time we recall an event, we partly rebuild it, and in the process details can be lost, changed or even added.

In a famous series of experiments, participants watched a film of a car accident and were then asked how fast the cars were going when they ''smashed into'' or ''hit'' each other. Those who heard the word ''smashed'' gave higher speed estimates, and a week later they were more likely to report seeing broken glass — although there had been none in the film. A single word had quietly reshaped the memory.

Later studies went further, showing that some people could be led to ''remember'' entire childhood events that had never taken place, such as being lost in a shopping centre, especially when the suggestion came from a trusted family member. Importantly, these false memories often felt just as vivid and certain as real ones.

These findings have serious implications for the legal system, which has traditionally placed great weight on the confident testimony of eyewitnesses. As a result, many police forces have changed how they interview witnesses, avoiding leading questions. The research does not mean that memory is worthless; most of what we remember is broadly accurate. It means that confidence alone is not proof of accuracy.', NULL, '{"A": "Memory is generally reliable, but feeling sure does not guarantee accuracy.", "B": "Memory is completely unreliable.", "C": "Courts should stop using any evidence from memory.", "D": "False memories are impossible to create.", "E": "Confident witnesses are always correct."}'::jsonb, 'A', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «Memory is generally reliable, but feeling sure does not guarantee accuracy.».');

Walk through the great museums of Europe and North America and you will see objects from every part of the world: sculptures, manuscripts, jewellery, religious items. Some were bought or exchanged fairly; others were taken during wars or colonial rule, often without the consent of the people who made or owned them. In recent years, demands for the return of such objects have grown louder.

Supporters of return, often called restitution, argue that these objects are not just works of art but parts of a community''s identity and history. Keeping them abroad, they say, continues an injustice, and the communities they came from have the right to decide how they are displayed and interpreted.

Those who hesitate raise different concerns. Some museum directors argue that large international collections allow millions of visitors to compare cultures side by side. Others worry about whether objects will be stored safely, or point out that it is not always clear who the rightful owner is when borders and states have changed over centuries.

In practice, the debate is moving away from simple yes-or-no answers. Several museums have returned specific objects after careful research into their history, while others have agreed to long-term loans, shared research projects or joint exhibitions. What seems to be changing most is the starting question: no longer only ''Is this object legally ours?'' but also ''How did it come to be here, and who has a stronger connection to it?''', NULL, '{"A": "Some were taken during wars or colonial rule without consent.", "B": "They were made by museum staff.", "C": "All were donated by their makers.", "D": "They were found in museum storerooms.", "E": "All were bought at fair prices."}'::jsonb, 'A', NULL, 'Cavab mətnə əsaslanır: «Some were taken during wars or colonial rule without consent.».');

